 0.75, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.25, "baseline_margin": 3.09, "analyst_adjustment": -1.09, "key_factors": ["Redistricting turned this formerly safe Democratic seat into an R+5 battleground", "Davis holds a large cash advantage ($3.6M to $1.8M) and a centrist record suited to the rural east", "Ratings split: Cook and Inside Elections call it Lean R for a flip; most others say Tossup or Lean D", "Markets price Davis near 60%; the lone recent poll (D-sponsored) shows him up seven"], "rationale": "The redrawn Republican-leaning lines offset Davis's incumbency and fundraising edge, leaving raters split between Lean R and Lean D around a Tossup consensus. Markets price Davis near 60 percent and the only recent poll shows him ahead, supporting a small Democratic edge in a high-uncertainty race.", "watch": "A heavier wave of Republican outside spending behind Buckhout, or a shift in the national midterm environment, could quickly move this tossup either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Don Davis (D) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 1st District became a genuine battleground after redrawing shifted it from a Democratic-leaning seat to one that favors Republicans by about five points. Don Davis, the incumbent, is running on a centrist record that polls well in the rural east, while Laurie Buckhout, an Army veteran and businesswoman, argues the new lines reflect the district's true tilt. National raters are split: Cook and Inside Elections call it Lean R for a flip, while several others rate it a Tossup or Lean D.\n\nMoney favors Davis, who has $3.6 million on hand to Buckhout's $1.8 million, though Republican outside groups have spent more than $800,000 supporting her and the DCCC is now on air attacking her record. The only recent poll, taken for Democratic allies in mid-September, showed Davis up seven. Prediction markets put his chances near 60 percent, leaving this race on the Democratic side of a genuine tossup.", "history": [{"d